It's usually all of the above.  I don't have access to a
compiler that conforms to the C++ standard, and C++ has enough
undefined behavior that I'm never sure my own code fully
conforms (and I probably know standard C++ better than most, and
take more care than most in making my code conform).

> The GNU C++ compiler is free, can target just about any
> platform that can accommodate Nethack 3.4.3 (yes, including
> Windows), and has a significant number of standards-adherence
> martinets on the development team.

And yet, it is not standard conformant.  Not even C++98,
completely.  (In the meantime, the official standard is C++03,
and there should soon be an even newer version.)  And code which
worked with g++ 3.x doesn't necessarily work with g++ 4.x
(because they are trying to improve standard compliance).  And
I've yet to be able to get a clean build of anything later than
4.1 on my platforms.

Realistically, if we did C++, I'd insist that all code compile
with all g++ versions since 2.95.2, VC++ since 6.0, Sun CC since
5.1...  If you expect your users to be able to compile the code,
you can't require the latest version, nor any particular
compiler.

